	<!-- IntroText -->
	<string name="intro_text">After installing AF, you must manually add the widget to your homescreen: When setting up a widget for the first time, the only thing which needs to be chosen is the location to view. The most recently saved settings will be used automatically when setting up a new widget. </string>

	<!-- Temperature graph -->
	<string name="temperature_graph_text">The temperature graph shows the temperature for the coming 24 hours, starting at the next full hour from the current time. NB:
		For areas where data is only available in 3-hour intervals, the time axis will be aligned to match the available data.\n
	</string>

	<!-- Precipitation -->
	<string name="precipitation_text">Precipitation is shown as blue bars, where the height of the bar indicates the amount of precipitation.
		Each grid cell is a given amount of precipitation, as indicated in the top bar. Normally, each cell will be 1 mm, or 0.05 inches.
		This means that a bar which is four cells high equates to four millimetres, or 0.2 inches of precipitation.\n

		\nTo indicate the maximum and minimum amount of forecasted precipitation, both filled bars and patterned bars are used.
		Patterned blue bars indicate the maximum amount of precipitation, while the filled blue bars indicate the minimum amount of precipitation.
	</string>

	<!-- Top bar information -->
	<string name="top_bar_information_text">The top bar of the widget is used to indicate information for the current time.\n

    	\n\u2022 The current mean wind and gust speed are given in km/h. When wind is hidden, the Mean-Sea-Level-Pressure (MSLP) is shown instead, in hectopascals.
    	\n\u2022 Relative Humidity (RH) is given as a percentage.
    	\n\u2022 The number to the right of the relative humidity is the precipitation scaling, as described in the previous paragraph.
    	\n\u2022 The number in the parenthesis after the location name indicates the current temperature at that location.
	</string>

	<!-- Wind and gusts -->
	<string name="wind_help_text">Weather data comes from the Météo-France models (AROME, then ARPEGE) through Open-Meteo.\n

    	\n\u2022 The solid line is the mean wind speed, the dashed line is the gust speed, both in km/h with the scale on the right side of the graph.
    	\n\u2022 Above the strong wind threshold (40 km/h by default), the lines change color and a dotted line marks the threshold.\n

		\nThe threshold and the colors can be changed in the appearance settings, under Wind.
	</string>

